      "source": [
        "### Exercise A.3 – Analogies with your model"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 9,
      "id": "c86516aa",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c86516aa",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "3b492f50-e4af-468e-8529-4fc3ee0ac888"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "doctor is to treatment as space is to: [('cooperation', 0.5705044269561768), ('british', 0.5621291995048523), ('coordination', 0.5602559447288513)]\n",
            "earth is to moon as sun is to: [('sparcstation', 0.653184175491333), ('sparc', 0.6376811265945435), ('dec', 0.6152940988540649)]\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "def analogy(model, a, b, c, topn=3):\n",
        "    \"\"\"a is to b as c is to ?   (vector arithmetic: b - a + c)\"\"\"\n",
        "    try:\n",
        "        return model.wv.most_similar(positive=[b, c], negative=[a], topn=topn)\n",
        "    except KeyError as e:\n",
        "        return f\"Word not in vocabulary: {e}\"\n",
        "\n",
        "# Try 2 analogies built from words of YOUR vocabulary\n",
        "print(\"doctor is to treatment as space is to:\", analogy(model_skipgram, \"doctor\", \"treatment\", \"space\"))\n",
        "print(\"earth is to moon as sun is to:\", analogy(model_skipgram, \"earth\", \"moon\", \"sun\"))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "5a2732f2",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5a2732f2"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Question A.4 (written)\n",
        "\n",
        "Did the analogies work? If yes, why does the result make sense; if not, what explains the failure (corpus size, vocabulary, frequency)?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "6ebb5ba8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "6ebb5ba8"
      },
      "source": [
        "<!-- ANSWER lab3.2-A4 -->\n",
        "**Your answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Analogy 1: \"doctor is to treatment as space is to...\" (Success)**\n",
        "   - **Result:** `frontier` (similarity: 0.582) and `civilian` (similarity: 0.575).\n",
        "   - **Why it worked:** This analogy worked remarkably well given the domain of our training corpus. In the medical sub-corpus, `doctor` and `treatment` share a highly functional, semantic relationship. When applied to the `sci.space` domain, the model successfully mapped this vector relationship to find `frontier` (as in \"space frontier\") and `civilian` (as in \"civilian space program\").\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Analogy 2: \"earth is to moon as sun is to...\" (Failure)**\n",
        "   - **Result:** Computer-hardware terms such as `sgi`, `sparc`, and `dec`.\n",
        "   - **Why it failed:** This failure is explained by **corpus composition and domain imbalance**. While `earth` and `moon` appear in `sci.space` posts, the term `sun` in our specific corpus overwhelmingly refers to **Sun Microsystems** (the workstation company) rather than the star. Because `comp.graphics` hardware discussions are heavily represented, the vector for `sun` was positioned close to other computer architectures and unix workstation manufacturers (`sgi`, `sparc`, `dec`). Thus, the mathematical analogy vector arithmetic was pulled directly into the hardware cluster instead of astronomical bodies.\n",
        "<!-- END ANSWER -->"
      ]
    },
