      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "# Part D – Visualizing embeddings with t-SNE"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 16,
      "id": "c734b9d9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c734b9d9",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 532
        },
        "outputId": "0aaa1148-6461-42a2-8fd7-eafc60588cd1"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 1000x700 with 1 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.manifold import TSNE\n",
        "\n",
        "embeddings_2d = TSNE(n_components=2, random_state=42, perplexity=10).fit_transform(doc_embeddings)\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 7))\n",
        "for category in my_categories:\n",
        "    mask = np.array(sampled_labels) == category\n",
        "    plt.scatter(embeddings_2d[mask, 0], embeddings_2d[mask, 1], label=category, s=90, alpha=0.8)\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.title(\"Document embeddings (MiniLM + t-SNE)\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "f5dfe0ca",
        "outputId": "12d9bd0f-1d7d-4d1e-e6f0-4a85b1742b11"
      },
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score\n",
        "\n",
        "# Calculate silhouette score in the 2D t-SNE projection space\n",
        "sil_score = silhouette_score(embeddings_2d, sampled_labels)\n",
        "print(f\"Average silhouette score for the 2D t-SNE clusters: {sil_score:.3f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "f5dfe0ca",
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Average silhouette score for the 2D t-SNE clusters: 0.314\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "211832c5",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "211832c5"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Question D.1 (written)\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Do the categories form distinct clusters?\n",
        "2. Are there documents in the \"wrong\" cluster? What could explain it?\n",
        "3. Which two categories are the closest? Does it match Lab 3.1?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "c0589c92",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c0589c92"
      },
      "source": [
        "<!-- ANSWER lab3.2-D1 -->\n",
        "**Your answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Do the categories form distinct clusters?**\n",
        "   - Yes, they do form visible clusters, but they are not completely isolated. The average silhouette score of **0.314** supports this observation; a positive score shows that documents are generally closer to their own cluster members than to other clusters, but a value around 0.3 indicates significant overlap, boundary mixing, or sub-clusters rather than highly separated compact spheres.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Are there documents in the \"wrong\" cluster? What could explain it?**\n",
        "   - Yes, a few points of one category lie closer to another category's core cluster (e.g., a blue `sci.space` point appearing down near the orange `comp.graphics` cluster).\n",
        "   - **Explanation:** This happens when a document belongs nominally to one class but heavily shares vocabulary, topics, or phrasing with another. For example, a `sci.space` post talking about \"NASA's computer visualization systems for space shuttles\" deals with space but shares significant technical terminology with `comp.graphics`. Sentence-Transformers capture these shared semantic contexts, pulling the document closer to the computer graphics cluster.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Which two categories are the closest? Does it match Lab 3.1?**\n",
        "   - Visually, **`sci.space`** and **`comp.graphics`** show the closest boundary interface and proximity, while **`sci.med`** forms a slightly more distinct cluster to the right.\n",
        "   - Yes, this closely matches the observations from Lab 3.1. Discussions in both space exploration and computer graphics share computational, mathematical, and physical visualization vocabularies (e.g., coordinates, processing, modeling), bringing their overall semantics closer to each other than to medical discussions.\n",
        "<!-- END ANSWER -->"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "ac6b72a9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ac6b72a9"
      },
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "# Part E – Final comparison\n",
        "\n",
        "### Question E.1 (written)\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Fill in the table.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | Strengths | Weaknesses | Best use case |\n",
        "|--------|-----------|------------|---------------|\n",
        "| BoW | | | |\n",
        "| TF-IDF | | | |\n",
        "| Word2Vec (own corpus) | | | |\n",
        "| GloVe | | | |\n",
        "| Sentence embeddings (MiniLM) | | | |\n",
        "\n",
        "2. For your categories, which method worked best overall? Support the answer with results from Labs 3.1 and 3.2.\n",
        "3. For a real classification system on these categories, which representation would you choose and why?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "634e489e",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "634e489e"
      },
      "source": [
        "<!-- ANSWER lab3.2-E1 -->\n",
        "**Your answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Comparison Table\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | Strengths | Weaknesses | Best use case |\n",
        "|--------|-----------|------------|---------------|\n",
        "| **BoW** | • Simple, highly explainable.<br>• Extremely fast to construct.<br>• No training required. | • Ignores word order and context.<br>• Suffers from extreme data sparsity.<br>• No semantic representation. | • Quick baseline classification.<br>• Basic keyword matching. |\n",
        "| **TF-IDF** | • Highlights domain-specific terms.<br>• Automatically demotes common words.<br>• Unsupervised and simple. | • Fails to handle synonyms/polysemy.<br>• Still ignores syntactic word order.<br>• Struggles with out-of-vocabulary (OOV) terms. | • Traditional text retrieval.<br>• Text classification on vocabulary-rich datasets. |\n",
        "| **Word2Vec (own corpus)** | • Captures domain-specific jargon well.<br>• Vocabulary matches corpus perfectly. | • Requires a very large corpus to work.<br>• Cannot handle OOV words at test time.<br>• One static vector representation per word. | • specialized text applications with unique vocabulary (e.g., medicine). |\n",
        "| **GloVe** | • Pre-trained on giant corpora (rich semantics).<br>• Solves small data limitations.<br>• Captures structural word analogies well. | • Out-of-vocabulary terms fail.<br>• Still static (cannot contextualize words like *bank* or *sun* in context). | • General NLP tasks where custom corpus size is constrained. |\n",
        "| **Sentence embeddings (MiniLM)** | • Captures context, synonymy, and deep semantics.<br>• Dense representations are excellent for distances.<br>• Handles unseen vocab via subwords. | • Computationally heavier (Transformer model inference).<br>• Harder to explain feature-level importance. | • Semantic search, Q&A systems, document clustering, and semantic deduplication. |\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Best Method for Our Categories\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, **Sentence Embeddings (MiniLM)** worked best for our specific categories (`sci.space`, `comp.graphics`, and `sci.med`).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Evidence from Labs 3.1 & 3.2:**\n",
        "- In Lab 3.1, the TF-IDF representation yielded a heatmap with low overall similarities and high sparsity. Unless documents shared exactly identical keywords, their cosine similarities remained close to zero, often misclassifying domain overlaps.\n",
        "- In Lab 3.2, the sentence embedding heatmap successfully formed highly visible, cohesive yellow/light-green blocks along the diagonal. The t-SNE plot grouped the 3 categories into visible clusters, achieving a **silhouette score of 0.314** directly in the 2D projection space.\n",
        "- The semantic search test illustrated that MiniLM correctly matches complex queries (like \"medical treatment and doctor options\") directly with highly relevant documents without requiring any explicit overlapping keywords.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Recommendation for a Real Classification System\n",
        "\n",
        "For a production-grade system on these categories, I would choose **Sentence Embeddings (MiniLM)** coupled with a downstream classifier (such as a Logistic Regression or a lightweight MLP classifier).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why:**\n",
        "1. **Semantic Robustness:** Online forum or helpdesk queries are highly informal and frequently use synonyms rather than the exact jargon found in technical text. While TF-IDF would completely miss these concepts, MiniLM understands the underlying semantic content.\n",
        "2. **Low Complexity Deployment:** `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` is extremely fast and small (only ~80MB) yet delivers accuracy comparable to much larger LLMs. This makes it highly cost-effective and practical to run in CPU or GPU production environments.\n",
        "3. **Clustering Quality:** The distinct clusters generated in our t-SNE projection demonstrate that these dense sentence representations naturally group documents by topic, which makes downstream classification tasks significantly easier and more accurate.\n",
        "<!-- END ANSWER -->"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "5d039e17",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5d039e17"
      },
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "## Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "- Word2Vec learns word vectors from your corpus; CBOW and Skip-gram differ in what they predict.\n",
        "- Pre-trained vectors (GloVe) cover a large general vocabulary and support analogies.\n",
        "- Sentence-transformer models embed whole texts and enable semantic search without shared words.\n",
        "- t-SNE projects embeddings in 2-D to inspect how documents group together."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "71625136",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "71625136"
      },
      "source": [
